        text: '[IMPORTANT: User invoked the "ark" skill; follow its instructions. Full skill below.]\n\n# Ark\n\nThis skill is the only entry to Ark. It picks one playbook, loads it, and follows it.\n\n## Pick the playbook\n\nRead the first word of the arguments. It is the playbook name.\n\n|Name|Arguments|Playbook file|Who runs it|\n|---|---|---|---|\n|`chief`|none|`playbooks/chief.md`|The human opens it in an Orca terminal. It is Master Chief.|\n|`run-lead`|`<run>`|`playbooks/run-lead.md`|Master Chief starts it, one per run.|\n|`worker`|`<run> <step>`|`playbooks/worker.md`|A run lead starts it, one per step.|\n|`bootstrap`|none|`playbooks/bootstrap.md`|The human, once per project.|\n|`brainstorm`|none|`playbooks/brainstorm.md`|The human opens it. It is the brainstorm partner and writes proposed tickets.|\n|`retro <run>`|`<run>`|`playbooks/retro.md`, Part 1: Close check|The run lead writes `retro.md`, then Master Chief asks the human three questions.|\n|`retro interview`|`interview`|`playbooks/retro.md`, Part 2: Deep interview|Master Chief, on request or every few runs.|\n\n## Steps\n\n1. Take the first word of the arguments as the name.\n2. If the name is missing or is not one of the six above, or if it is `retro` without `<run>` or `interview`, do nothing else. Print this list and stop:\n\n   ```text\n   Unknown playbook "<name>". Use one of:\n     chief                    Master Chief: evaluate, queue and supervise tickets\n     run-lead <run>           Run lead: plan and drive one run\n     worker <run> <step>      Worker: do one step of a run\n     bootstrap                Teach Ark a new project\n     brainstorm               Brainstorm partner: write proposed tickets with the human\n     retro <run>              Close check for one run\n     retro interview          Deep interview across several runs\n   ```\n\n3. Check that the playbook has the arguments it needs. `run-lead` needs a run name, `worker` needs a run name and a step name, and `retro` needs a run name or the word `interview`. If one is missing, print the usage line from the table and stop.\n4. Read the playbook file from this skill\'s folder, in full.\n5. Follow it. Pass the remaining arguments to it as `<run>` and `<step>`.\n\n## Rules for every playbook\n\n- The `ark` command does the repeatable work: run state, worktrees, tests, MRs and metrics. Never edit `events.ndjson` or `state.json` by hand.\n- Answers at a gate are the human\'s decisions. An agent never answers a gate for the human.\n- Master Chief and run leads never write product code. Only an implementer worker does.\n- Run folders live under `$ARK_HOME/projects/<project>/runs/<run>/`. `ARK_HOME` defaults to `~/.ark`.\n\n---\n\n[Skill directory: /Users/cobekarhoff/.omp/plugins/node_modules/ark/skills/ark]\nResolve relative paths in this skill (e.g. `scripts/foo.js`, `templates/config.yaml`) against this absolute directory; read referenced assets and templates; run scripts with the terminal tool when skill instructions call for it.\nUser: You are working inside Orca, a multi-agent IDE. You are a dispatched worker.\nYour coordinator\'s Orca session ID is: orca_session_id:ec2e2c31-b641-4a83-889c-8c5c4e0b8566\nYour task ID is: task_fd4f6f1fe1da\nYour Orca session ID is: orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c\n\nThe coordinator cannot see this chat. Reach it with the `orca orchestration` commands\nbelow, and message it only to ask, report a blocker, or finish; it needs no progress updates\nunless the task asks.\nDon\'t post to Slack, GitHub, or other channels during the run.\nDo only this task, yourself: no subagents or helper workers unless the task asks.\n\n=== CLI COMMANDS ===\n\n```sh\n  # Ask the coordinator, then end your turn; Orca wakes you with the answer.\n  # Never use a local question prompt such as AskUserQuestion; the\n  # coordinator cannot see or answer it.\n  orca orchestration ask --from orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c --question "<your question>"\n\n  # Blocked, and the coordinator must act before you can continue:\n  orca orchestration send --from orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c --type escalation --subject "Blocked: <reason>" --body "<details>" --task-id task_fd4f6f1fe1da --dispatch-id ctx_5e2ad9697dee\n\n  # Finish: send exactly once. --body is three sentences: what you did, what\n  # you found, what is left. Use --outcome failed if the work is not done;\n  # never hide failure in prose. Add --files-modified or --report-path only\n  # with real values. You are done when it confirms; if it fails, follow the\n  # recovery it prints.\n  orca orchestration send --from orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c --type worker_done --subject "<short status>" --body "<3-sentence summary: what you did, what you found, what\'s left>" --task-id task_fd4f6f1fe1da --dispatch-id ctx_5e2ad9697dee --outcome succeeded\n```\n\n=== AFTER YOU SEND worker_done ===\n\nworker_done ends your turn for this task: return to an idle prompt. Do NOT\nstart other work, do NOT run a sleep/poll loop, and do NOT keep calling\n`orca orchestration check`.\n\nA direct instruction from the user takes precedence over this idle rule: it is\nnew work, so do it without the settled task or Dispatch IDs.\nNever refuse a direct user request because you were a worker.\n\nMore supervised work arrives in this chat as a fresh preamble and TASK\nblock; follow it under its new Dispatch.\n\n=== SUB-DISPATCH ===\nIf the task asks for helper workers, start them here. They report to you: their\nquestions, escalations, and worker_done arrive in your own `check`, next to\nyour coordinator\'s mail.\n\n```sh\n  orca orchestration worker-start --from orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c --spec "<sub-task>" --worktree current --agent <agent> --json\n  # Answer a helper\'s question by its message ID:\n  orca orchestration reply --from orca_session_id:6330a163-0629-48b1-99f8-b1596db09d6c --id <message_id> --body "<answer>"\n```\n\nWait until every helper has settled (worker_done, a failed start, stop, or\nabandon) before reporting your own, and name any that failed. Nesting is\ncapped; your helpers may not be able to start helpers of their own.\n\n---\n\n=== TASK === worker 2026-11-06-ARK-313-1 test-case-design\nYou are the VALIDATOR for step test-case-design of run 2026-11-06-ARK-313-1.\nThis step writes NO test code. It writes test cases in full before the implementer starts: fill test/cases/ARK-T16.md and test/cases/ARK-T17.md (already allocated; keep ids) with objective, preconditions, steps, expected results, risk, technique, level, priority and screenshot checkpoints, as in test/cases/ARK-T10.md. Cases are in plan.yml test_cases. Do not write wording tests: playbook/doc wording is proved by inspection. Commit the files.',
